WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.180
What’s up guys, Helen here.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.119
At Wisecrack there’s nothing we like more
than having our minds bent by movies.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:24.249
Whether we’re wrapping our brains around
multiple layers of dreamland faux-reality,

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:28.849
witnessing a ballerina totally losing her
shit, or watching a dude explore the intricacies

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:33.180
of indoor plumbing, we tend to absolutely
love it when our movies turn our brains into

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.180
jello.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.590
And arguably, few films are quite as mind-bendy
as Martin Scorcese’s 2010 thriller, Shutter

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:40.630
Island.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:44.250
The film has more twists and turns than a
Chuck-E-Cheese playground slide, but we’ve

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.660
always found the reveal at the end to be more
than vaguely predictable.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:52.050
In the late 90s and early 2000s a lot of movies
featured a twist revealing our protagonist’s

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.270
warped perspective: Fight Club, A Beautiful
Mind, The Machinist, Identity, etc.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.430
Coming out in 2010, it seemed like Shutter
Island was a little late to the party, leaving

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.390
us at Wisecrack wondering...

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.390
Is that all there is to it?

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:09.149
Is this movie as smart as you’d expect from
one of America's most celebrated filmmakers?

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:10.939
Is there something deeper going on?

00:01:10.939 --> 00:01:45.369
Let’s find out in this Wisecrack Edition
on Shutter Island: Deep

00:01:45.369 --> 00:02:06.259
or Dumb?

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:56.189
Oh and 
for the second time - spoilers ahead.

00:02:56.189 --> 00:03:01.360
Alright guys let’s do a little recap: Shutter
Island places us in a creepy mental institution

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.700
made creepier by its convenient isolation
from society vis-a-vis a shit-ton of water.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.790
Leo plays ugly-tie-wearing Teddy, a US Marshall
investigating the disappearance of Rachel

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:16.970
Solando, one of the island’s dangerous criminal
inmates whose favorite hobbies include drowning

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.879
her children and then blacking out.

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:24.700
Soon, we meet the requisite sketchy psychiatrists,
including one who’s definitely Nazi-curious.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.980
When head shrink Dr. Cawley proves shady and
doesn’t cooperate with the investigation,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.590
things seem increasingly more sinister.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.799
All the while, Teddy has a ton of trippy flashbacks,
reliving the various traumas of his life ranging

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:40.739
from liberating the Dachau concentration camp
to losing his wife to a fire.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:46.129
Also, turns out that the guy who started said
fire, Andrew Leidis, is at this mental institution

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:50.090
and Teddy wants to track him down.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.760
This whole mission culminates in him “discovering”
the “real” Rachel Solando hanging out

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.730
by an international rat convention, claiming
to be a psychiatrist who discovered that the

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:03.109
doctors are using lobotomies and experiments
as part of a top-secret government program

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.629
to develop mind control techniques.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:06.629
Yikes!

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:11.140
Only things are about to get wilder: we find
out that Teddy is actually Andrew, who became

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.700
psychotic after killing his wife because she
killed their three children.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.940
On top of that, his entire investigation was
actually just an elaborate LARPing experiment

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.770
conducted by the asylum to help rid Teddy
-- I mean Andrew… um, I’m just gonna call

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:28.630
him Teddy, okay?-- of his illusions as a slightly-demented
form of role-play therapy while presumably

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:33.220
also giving this doctor &amp; patient amateur
acting troupe a chance to show off their chops.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.550
Oh also, unless Leo acknowledges the truth
about his past, and accepts the totality of

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.710
his identity, he’s going to be lobotomized.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.281
He’s like “hard pass” and heads up to
the creepy lighthouse to go get his brain

00:04:43.281 --> 00:04:44.530
jiggled by chopsticks.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:45.740
The end!

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:50.240
Even if you’ve seen Shutter Island, that
synopsis might read like your most ADHD friend

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:51.979
trying to get through a campfire story.

00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:56.400
It’s helpful here to contextualize this
movie as an homage to high and lowbrow film

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.770
noir traditions.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:02.050
Film noir was the catch-all phrase for this
special brand of film that particularly flourished

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.669
post World-War 2.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:09.810
It featured dark shadows, unreliable narrators,
weird-angled shots, and perhaps most iconically:

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:15.699
plots that involve plenty of unexpected twists
and dramatic reveals, but maybe aren’t 100%

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:16.699
airtight.

00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:19.259
All of the above could just as easily describe
Shutter Island.

00:05:19.259 --> 00:05:24.449
Indeed, this film is almost pulpy in the way
it purely revels in every noir trope - from

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:30.030
the creepy cemetery to the fantastical dream
sequences to the narrator with the slowly-revealed

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.400
“dark past,” especially one rooted in
World War II.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:38.900
With mental asylums, a looming tall building
- in this case a lighthouse, creepy staircases,

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:44.270
casual semi-Nazis, and more, the film almost
reads like a grab-bag of noir traditions.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:48.590
That Scorcese was deliberately evoking noir
is important in parsing the film.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:53.080
That’s because, in general, noir films are
less concerned with narrative coherence than

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.140
they are with mood, atmosphere, and conveying
the psychological condition, usually suffering,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.599
of their protagonist.

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:03.581
We’re going to treat Shutter Island like
a noir and say upfront: The narrative plot

00:06:03.581 --> 00:06:07.140
has a lot of turns and arguably doesn’t
add up.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.000
But that’s almost literally irrelevant.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.000
What’s more interesting is whether the film
compellingly depicts Teddy’s psychological

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.110
condition, and in the process, reveals anything
about us slash humanity at large.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.680
So with that caveat, let’s dive in.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.139
At its core, Shutter Island is about a man
who refuses to acknowledge large swaths of

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.789
his inner self and so he creates an alternate
reality.

00:06:28.789 --> 00:06:37.310
Scholar Stella Bruzzi actually compares it
to Inception when she writes that:

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:50.560
Indeed, Teddy’s closed himself off from
the traumas of his past, and can only reckon

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.569
with those memories by dreaming them or projecting
them onto other people’s lives - hence conjuring

00:06:55.569 --> 00:06:57.289
Rachel Solando and Leidis.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:02.830
He’s living in a fantasy world lurking with
sinister, presumably imagined, threats.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.680
His disconnection with his own identity is
almost immediately established in one of the

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:11.430
first shots - when we see Teddy examining
his own face in a shaky mirror.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.270
He’s just puked his guts out, suggesting
an inner discordance and inability to orient

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.270
himself.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.940
The mirror perhaps indicates the duplicity
inherent to his character.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:28.740
As scholar Annette Wernblad puts it:

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.229
From there, Scorcese uses every tool in his
cinematic toolbox to convey this warped reality.

00:07:34.229 --> 00:07:39.479
Most literally, the film employs an estimated
650 special effects including blue screen

00:07:39.479 --> 00:07:43.290
mattes, digital painting, 3D computer effects
and even digital miniatures.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:47.939
While plenty of movies use a crap ton of special
effects, they’re generally employed to achieve

00:07:47.939 --> 00:07:49.280
visual believability.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.080
Here, all of the special effects work to create
a slightly artificial reality.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.379
This is fitting for a movie in which 90% of
the action takes place inside a giant, performative

00:07:58.379 --> 00:08:01.810
charade undertaken by the world’s most dramatically
talented shrinks.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:05.910
It’s even more fitting for a narrative viewed
entirely through the eyes of a man literally

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.810
living in a fantasy world.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:12.580
Examples of this artificiality include the
rear projection inspired of the ocean and

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:17.280
sky behind the boat when the men arrive at
Shutter Island, the artificial lushishness

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.020
of this gazebo setting, and the almost absurdly-thick,
dream-like layer of fog in the movie’s opening

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:24.020
shot.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:28.800
At the same time, the perspective is purposefully
disjointed, with instances of almost deliberately

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.830
sloppy editing, like in the boat sequence
when Teddy’s partner’s mouth is out of

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:37.280
sync with his words until it cuts to a reverse
shot, at which point it’s suddenly in sync:

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.040
“How long you been with the marshalls?”

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.860
“4 Years”
“So you know how small it is?”

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:45.860
“Sure.”

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:48.370
“How ‘bout you?

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.370
You got a girl?”

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:53.360
A similar effect is achieved in the first
flashback, when Teddy’s wife starts talking

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.649
before we see her mouth move, followed by
another jarring cut:

00:08:57.649 --> 00:09:09.091
“Remember when we stayed in the cabin in
the summer, Teddy?”

00:09:09.091 --> 00:09:10.091
“We were so happy.”

00:09:10.091 --> 00:09:11.091
“She’s here, you can’t leave.”

00:09:11.091 --> 00:09:13.200
Here, the contrasting use of jarring angles
in succession, along with inaccurate sound

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.870
synching, serves to diorient us and rattle
our perspective, an imitation of Teddy’s

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.060
rattled psyche.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:22.589
In another instance, when the men are being
introduced to the buildings on the island

00:09:22.589 --> 00:09:27.029
- there’s a whip pan which sloppily hides
an editing cut, as if Teddy hesitates for

00:09:27.029 --> 00:09:30.959
a moment in keeping up the charade while staring
at familiar buildings.

00:09:30.959 --> 00:09:35.620
All of these interruptions are entirely intentional
- meant to jolt us, just as Teddy’s psyche

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.740
is being jolted with memories.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.890
The sound-design also reflects Teddy's heightened
sense of fear and paranoia.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:47.310
Sometimes, the sound is too loud, as with
the initial over-the-top ship noises that

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:52.519
don’t coincide with the actual waves, creating
a sense of hyperreality fitting for a character

00:09:52.519 --> 00:10:08.110
with no grasp on the truth.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:12.470
Other times the sound is unnaturally quiet,
as when they’re entering Ward C, and we

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:19.810
see a lot more rain than we can hear.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:27.070
Throughout, during moments of high tension,
sounds and music are ramped up beyond mere

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:35.880
dramatics - from the extreme introduction
to the island to the thunderstorm that booms

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:42.959
after Teddy gets a disturbing note from one
of the patients.

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:46.250
Other visual elements help situate us in Teddy’s
mind.

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:53.990
As the film’s director of photography, Robert
Richardson, put it:

00:10:53.990 --> 00:11:02.010
Colors in particular play an important role
from the very beginning.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:06.380
Teddy’s false reality on Shutter Island
is desaturated and vaguely dismal.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:10.731
These segments are far less vivid than his
fantasies and dreams, which take place in

00:11:10.731 --> 00:11:15.260
hyper-saturated hues inspired by 1950s-era
Kodachrome film.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:19.339
That they feel hyperreal suggests that Teddy’s
dreamworld is more palpable to him than the

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:24.110
world around him, and helps us understand
just how vivid his delusions really are.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:28.520
While much of the present-day scenes are filled
with gloomy grays and blues, it doesn’t

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.589
stay this way the entire movie - The colors
become increasingly brighter, almost blinding,

00:11:33.589 --> 00:11:37.570
in moments when Teddy comes close to discovering
the truth or has an encounter that jiggles

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:43.029
his memory - crescendoing in this scene, which
is filled with flashes of light, perhaps literally

00:11:43.029 --> 00:11:46.740
representing the way the truth will “shine
a light” on Teddy’s delusions.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:52.389
In fact, Teddy even walks right into the light
as his doctor delivers the final blow of reality,

00:11:52.389 --> 00:11:57.630
showing him photos of his dead children,and
he falls into a halo of brightness and collapses

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:00.090
after remembering these painful experiences.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:05.710
In the final scene, the grounds of the asylum
are nearly as saturated as his fantasies,

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:09.490
suggesting that he is fully, or at least more
so, present in reality.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:14.199
Scrocese doesn’t only rely on the strength
of his own movie to convey this theme.

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:19.180
See, Scorcese is a Grade-A film nerd, and
that nerdiness is on full display in Shutter

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:24.550
Island: The film is littered with references
to other movies, especially film noirs, but

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:29.550
also other classics about madness and psychosis
like Suspiria and Shock Corridor.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:34.670
His clearest influence, though, is Alfred
Hitchcok, who popularized the modern use of

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:39.250
the “psychological subjectivity via an unreliable
narrator” trope.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:43.160
Scorcese even had his crew watch Hitchcock’s
“The Wrong Man” in preparation for shooting

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.600
Shutter Island.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:48.949
As the eagle-eyed Guardian writer Andrew Pulver
notes, these homages range from a shower head

00:12:48.949 --> 00:12:53.839
spouting water like in Psycho, to climbing
a narrow stairway like in Vertigo, to hanging

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:58.269
off of a cliff like Cary Grant, in North by
Northwest, to this creative shot of a gun

00:12:58.269 --> 00:13:02.040
like in Spellbound, to this tree flying through
a wall like in Marnie.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.250
What’s more, the use of bluescreen matte
paintings is reminiscent of the technology

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:10.460
Hitchcock would use when situating his characters
in unfilmable backgrounds.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:20.139
As the film’s special effects master Rob
Legato put it:

00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:23.510
Another famous director, Stanley Kubrick,
gets a shoutout too.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:28.050
The looming tracking shots that put us directly
in Teddy’s physical movements deliberately

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:32.519
evoke a similar technique that Kubrick innovatively
employed in The Shining as a means of more

00:13:32.519 --> 00:13:35.290
accurately conveying subjectivity through
cinematography.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:40.250
Similarly, the choice to use music by one
of Kubrick’s favored composers, György

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.480
Liget also evokes the master’s work.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.300
We could spend ages parsing the film for every
reference, but we think you get the point.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:51.550
Scorcese is infusing his film with all the
major cinematic trappings of subjectivity

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:55.360
and unreliable narration to put us directly
in Teddy’s shoes.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.500
So Scorcese is certainly using a lot of cool
techniques to make the movie more effective,

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:01.790
but does that mean that there's anything deep
there?

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:05.410
Well, we think so, and yep- we're going meta.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:09.930
All of these devices are actually reinforcing
a major theme of the film: that we need to

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:14.300
empathize with people experiencing mental
illness, rather than writing them off as crazy

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:15.389
or untreatable.

00:14:15.389 --> 00:14:19.190
This is Dr Cawley’s position to begin with,
that you should treat patients like human

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:23.470
beings and see them as capable of growth and
improvement, even if they’ve committed violent

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:24.470
crimes.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:25.930
Just as Kinglsey sees them as sympathetic,
saying:

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.459
“My job is to treat my patients, not their
victims.

00:14:28.459 --> 00:14:29.750
I’m not here to judge.”

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.930
We too learn to view them as sympathetic by
seeing the world through an actual patient’s

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:34.930
perspective.

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:38.990
At the beginning, these patients are regarded
by the camera as freaks and spectacles, to

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.730
be viewed from afar and nervously ignored.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:46.630
By the end, we’ve engaged with many of them
as human beings, and most importantly, we’ve

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.760
been thrust into Teddy’s subjectivity.

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:54.560
We’ve seen his struggle firsthand, thereby
making us recognize his unmistakable humanity.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.560
As a result, we know that, despite the whole
killing his wife thing, Teddy’s a pretty

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.589
caring dude with some genuinely good qualities.

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:07.149
If we had met Teddy as an inmate early on,
we would have regarded him as merely insane,

00:15:07.149 --> 00:15:10.330
not unlike Teddy does when he first surveys
the island’s patients.

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:14.690
The film seems to rather blatantly articulate
its thesis statement via this written plaque

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:19.010
at the beginning of the film, which reads:

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:25.269
We’re forced by nature of the cinematic
experience to sympathize with people whom

00:15:25.269 --> 00:15:27.960
society typically ignores or overlooks.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:33.459
Cinema’s one of the only art forms that
can so palpably immerse us in someone else’s

00:15:33.459 --> 00:15:38.200
experience, and we think that makes it a pretty
powerful thing, and Shutter Island a pretty

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.639
powerful film.

00:15:39.639 --> 00:15:44.470
But all of this is in the service of the central
question being posed by the film: Can you

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:47.350
rehabilitate evil, and should you even try?

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:52.760
Throughout the film, Teddy makes his position
very clear: Evil is black and white to him.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:57.399
He doesn’t see the inmates at Shutter Island
as worthy of reformation because they've committed

00:15:57.399 --> 00:15:59.009
violent crimes.

00:15:59.009 --> 00:16:01.990
“He’s… defenders, right?

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:06.269
They’ve hurt people, murdered them in some
cases.”

00:16:06.269 --> 00:16:08.130
“In almost all cases, yes.”

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:12.250
“And personally, doctor, I’d have to say
screw their sense of calm.”

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:17.410
He also refuses to give a dying Nazi a gun
so he can end the pain, and he’s the first

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:21.290
soldier to start shooting at the Nazis after
the Allies have raided the camp.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:27.329
In contrast, Dr. Cawley makes clear his opposing
view - that no person is beyond redemption

00:16:27.329 --> 00:16:29.180
or rehabilitation.

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:33.610
These two opposing views are complicated when
we learn that Teddy is in fact an inmate whom

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:36.350
Cawley is currently attempting to rehabilitate.

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:40.920
The film addresses this question most compellingly,
though, through its use of flashbacks to memories

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:44.190
that Teddy can’t process without dissociating
from.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:48.339
What the film seems to argue is that what
we consider “evil” is really just the

00:16:48.339 --> 00:16:51.180
sum result of trauma mixed with mental instability.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:56.319
We can recognize that Teddy isn’t evil,
and in doing so, have to question whether

00:16:56.319 --> 00:16:57.790
anybody really is.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:01.110
Except… hold the phone.. what about Nazis??

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:05.800
Well, what makes this film so interesting
is that it isn’t so one-sided on the issue

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:09.030
of evil - it leaves us to decide for ourselves.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:13.850
Teddy doesn’t reconcile his trauma and suddenly
believe that people can be rehabilitated.

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:14.850
Quite the opposite.

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:17.990
Instead, it seems like he chooses to be taken
to the lighthouse to be lobotomized.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:18.990
“Which would be worse?

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:25.950
To live as a monster or die as a good man?”

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:30.890
Here, he seems to be explaining his two choices:
Live with the knowledge of what he’s done

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:35.170
or metaphorically “die” vis a vis a stick
in his brain while still under the illusion

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:36.770
that he is a good guy.

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:41.200
Here, we see Teddy appearing to affirm his
belief stated throughout the movie - that

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:45.980
the violently criminal cannot be reformed,
that he is not capable of change or growing

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:47.680
past his mistakes.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:52.450
Upon close analysis, it becomes pretty clear
that every cinematic element - from lighting

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:58.180
to coloring to production design to cinematography
to music cues - was carefully chosen in order

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:02.730
to serve the higher themes at work - with
those being the subjectivity of film, the

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:08.020
nature of insanity, the redemptive potential
of the criminal and the very nature of evil.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:12.380
So we’re going to have to call this film
deep, and at least deeper than the deep end

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.020
of your community swimming pool.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.720
But what do you guys think?

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.220
Is Shutter Island a Scorcesian masterpiece?

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:22.100
Or did dude get a little too caught up in
faking hurricanes and generally fail to impress

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:23.660
with his “big twist” ending?

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:24.840
Let us know what you think in the comments.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.960
And, as always, thanks to our dope patrons
for supporting the channel and our podcasts.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:32.270
Remember to hit that subscribe button and,
as always, peace.

